WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.660
Film Courage: What does an outline look like?

00:00:01.660 --> 00:00:04.820
Mark Sanderson, Screenwriter/Author: Well
for me on these assignments that I do, they

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:10.250
are usually 10 or 12 pages and it’s the
entire story treatment.

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:14.050
And it’s not just line-by-line, I mean there
are some outlines where it’s just a line

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:20.000
and a line but for these movies for television
they are eight acts so everything has to be

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.000
broken down.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.000
Gordy Hoffman, Screenwriter/Founder of Blue
Cat Screenplay Competition: And then when

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.150
I started I was just like…I was literally
like “Oh, okay.”

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:31.490
And I knew something about page 30 and page
60 or whatever and I started to try to follow

00:00:31.490 --> 00:00:37.100
that and I didn’t find it difficult to remember
that something was supposed to happen but

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:39.760
I was very much on my own and I wrote whatever.

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:44.020
Mark: I write it as a story so if you were
to sit down and read it, you’d be reading

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:50.540
the story of the movie which I think is essential
to create which in fact I’ve had friends

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:53.770
that do this “I’m just going to sit down
and I have the vague idea and I’m just going

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:54.770
to write.”

00:00:54.770 --> 00:01:00.830
And I’m like “You’ll be lost in the
barren wasteland of Act 2 with no water, you

00:01:00.830 --> 00:01:04.619
be in the desert, you’ll be lost and you’ll
wonder how you’re going to trudge through

00:01:04.619 --> 00:01:05.779
those 75 pages.”

00:01:05.779 --> 00:01:09.749
Gordy: You know it’s very important for
people to remember you don’t have to learn

00:01:09.749 --> 00:01:11.229
the rules to break them.

00:01:11.229 --> 00:01:12.709
You can just ignore them.

00:01:12.709 --> 00:01:16.039
If you feel like you’re being expressive
and something is cool then you’re writing

00:01:16.039 --> 00:01:19.130
something that is cool and you start showing
it to other people and other people think

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:22.529
it’s cool then that’s the only thing we
care about.

00:01:22.529 --> 00:01:29.029
We would not have any Fellini, we would not
have Orson Welles, we probably would not have

00:01:29.029 --> 00:01:30.189
Kurosawa.

00:01:30.189 --> 00:01:35.849
Eventually people had to stop listening to
what people are suggesting and nowadays people

00:01:35.849 --> 00:01:42.539
being told they can’t direct, women, people
of color, diverse voices that have been discouraged

00:01:42.539 --> 00:01:46.119
or whatever, for all of those people I would
just be like “Get out of my face!

00:01:46.119 --> 00:01:51.349
I’m going to say what I want to say and
that’s the only way we move forward with

00:01:51.349 --> 00:01:56.219
this art form is when people just say “I
don’t care what I’m supposed to be doing

00:01:56.219 --> 00:02:01.049
or who is supposed to be directing or who
is supposed to be writing.

00:02:01.049 --> 00:02:05.209
The only time we move forward when people
are like “Well let’s follow it.

00:02:05.209 --> 00:02:07.360
On page 23 this is supposed to happen.

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:10.289
That’s when everyone wants to blow their
brains out.

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:12.099
I want something new.

00:02:12.099 --> 00:02:18.159
And that’s why things like streaming and
like episodic television did grow probably

00:02:18.159 --> 00:02:23.680
in response to formulaic feature writing,
it’s probably one of the reasons because

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:28.599
people were like “God can I watching something
else that doesn’t feel like everything else

00:02:28.599 --> 00:02:34.120
and it’s because of the formula and people
were following what they were supposed to

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:35.239
be doing.

00:02:35.239 --> 00:02:38.709
And it’s like not good!

00:02:38.709 --> 00:02:47.510
Mark: I’m a huge advocate of starting outlines
before starting writing and I know it’s

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:54.489
probably 50, 60, 70 percent of the work because
it makes the load a lot easier and you can

00:02:54.489 --> 00:02:57.569
write a faster screenplay if you have an outline.

00:02:57.569 --> 00:03:04.000
Now that doesn’t say there is no room for
changes or improvising but if you don’t

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:09.109
have a solid roadmap going in…it’s almost
like a pre-draft of a first draft.

00:03:09.109 --> 00:03:14.379
And I’m not an advocate of what people say
the vomit draft or just spill it out.

00:03:14.379 --> 00:03:18.930
But I don’t have the luxury of spilling
it out on my assignment jobs, I don’t.

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:27.489
I really have to turn in let’s say on a
ten scale turn in probably an eight…an eight

00:03:27.489 --> 00:03:33.409
out of ten…because I’m now holding up
development and I’ve also done rewrites

00:03:33.409 --> 00:03:41.629
on other screenwriter’s work/projects that
means that the script that they had went through

00:03:41.629 --> 00:03:44.849
multiple drafts and still is not there.

00:03:44.849 --> 00:03:51.120
And they have a buyer, they have network who
is waiting on the script and so I’ve also

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:56.450
been hired to do rewrite job where I can come
in but it’s like a page one rewrite where

00:03:56.450 --> 00:04:00.209
see the script that you have you can’t use
any of it, the names yes, the concept yes

00:04:00.209 --> 00:04:07.150
but you basically…but that’s something
that I’ve learned how to do which is good

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:14.499
because there is a lot of rewrite work out
there and some writers look down on it.

00:04:14.499 --> 00:04:20.440
An example is that I took a rewrite job and
that ended up being three more jobs for the

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:26.800
same company because that opened the door
for them and they were thankful for that.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:36.520
So you never know what opportunity you’re
going to either turn down or accept.

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:40.449
But back to your point about the outline,
I think it’s extremely important to do an

00:04:40.449 --> 00:04:44.830
outline before your screenplay because it’s
easier to work out the problems there than

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:49.250
it is writing a complete first draft and just
having so many problems.

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:55.169
And also it trains you like I said, like you’re
an olympic athlete, it trains you for the

00:04:55.169 --> 00:04:59.860
time that you do have to do outlines because
I’m not allowed to write these assignments

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:02.500
without an outline that I have to create.

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:04.030
So they just wont let me go to pages.

00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:07.250
They have other people involved who have to
okay it.

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:08.730
So the outline is extremely important.

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:14.050
Gordy: I think there are a lot of people who
get discouraged today because they do think

00:05:14.050 --> 00:05:19.640
they’re doing something wrong and there
is that element out there on messages board,

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:25.520
on podcasts, very popular podcasts that talk
about black and white and define things in

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:28.319
black and white and it’s dangerous.

00:05:28.319 --> 00:05:36.009
It’s like people have been trying to figure
out where this industry is going, where storytelling

00:05:36.009 --> 00:05:43.889
is going all the time, since I’ve been working
to write screenplays or whatever and I’ve

00:05:43.889 --> 00:05:47.330
seen all the different iterations of like
Well you’re not able to write.

00:05:47.330 --> 00:05:49.050
You shouldn’t write anything period.

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:50.950
You shouldn’t write anything about the industry.

00:05:50.950 --> 00:05:51.950
You should do this.

00:05:51.950 --> 00:05:52.950
You shouldn’t do that.

00:05:52.950 --> 00:05:54.330
And all of this has been blown up.

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:55.770
You shouldn’t write anything.

00:05:55.770 --> 00:06:04.650
I mean like an online bookseller, an online
bookstore wins Oscars now.

00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:12.750
Don’t tell me what anything black and white
is anymore, so it’s dangerous.

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:18.800
We can talk about the rules of why people
care about movies like sort of the laws of

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:23.011
emotion and I think no one is going to really
get freaked out about it.

00:06:23.011 --> 00:06:25.800
I think that’s sort of organic stuff.

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:29.180
But when I started off I read Michael Hauge’s
book.

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:37.220
And I don’t even remember…I don’t think
he talked a lot about formulas or beat sheets

00:06:37.220 --> 00:06:40.360
(he might have talked about it).

00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:45.650
The only thing I remember from Michael’s
book was encouragement, like he just empowers,

00:06:45.650 --> 00:06:49.160
he gave me hope, he definitely encouraged
me.

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:51.430
That’s what I always responded to.

00:06:51.430 --> 00:07:01.470
And he had the sense that it was rare for
something good to be written but he did find

00:07:01.470 --> 00:07:02.740
it possible.

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:06.229
It’s very much, it was a hopeful book.

00:07:06.229 --> 00:07:07.639
So that was the only thing I got.

00:07:07.639 --> 00:07:11.819
So it was the only screenwriting book that
I ever read a lot from (I don’t know if

00:07:11.819 --> 00:07:14.280
I read the whole thing through but I would
often refer to it).

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:15.349
Mark: it’s not that mysterious.

00:07:15.349 --> 00:07:23.389
There is a step outline, there’s a one sheet
which is just a one sheet of paper which is

00:07:23.389 --> 00:07:24.389
the concept.

00:07:24.389 --> 00:07:25.960
It’s sort of a short synopsis.

00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:30.720
There’s the log line which is even smaller
(two or three sentences).

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:36.050
So there are different steps and you have
to have each along the way because you have

00:07:36.050 --> 00:07:39.139
the quick pitch “Hmmm…I’m not interested.”

00:07:39.139 --> 00:07:41.009
Then you have the quick pitch and they go
“Hmmm?

00:07:41.009 --> 00:07:45.349
I’m willing to read something (not the script
but I’ll ready the synopsis).

00:07:45.349 --> 00:07:53.120
Then there are different steps as to how interested
they are to read what you want as the final

00:07:53.120 --> 00:08:01.430
product, the final script is for them to read.

00:08:01.430 --> 00:08:06.080
A step outline, I’ve done outlines that
were 30 pages.

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:10.250
I mean you triple it and you have the script.

00:08:10.250 --> 00:08:15.550
Everything had to be worked out because it
was very technical the way the film was going

00:08:15.550 --> 00:08:21.810
to be shot from like an iPhone and from different
things and so really it had to be spelled

00:08:21.810 --> 00:08:27.419
out because on the set you just can’t leave
it up to chance so.

00:08:27.419 --> 00:08:31.310
But after that point I could have written
a script in weeks because it was all figured

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:33.920
out, I’d figured it out already.

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:38.400
And you want that outline for those dark periods
when you’re stuck.

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:44.600
You don’t want to be stuck trying to figure
out plot points when you should be filling

00:08:44.600 --> 00:08:45.600
pages.

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:47.790
That’s the worst part, that’s the worst
place to be.

00:08:47.790 --> 00:08:53.420
Gordy: Audiences don’t care what page number
a scene is on.

00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:57.010
All the audience is responding to is the emotional
voracity.

00:08:57.010 --> 00:09:02.150
They want to be sucked in, they want to feel
a part of the Universe.

00:09:02.150 --> 00:09:07.880
People go into…have you ever thought about
this, people walk into a theater to watch

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:13.350
other people on a screen act out stuff from
life and they just watch it.

00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:14.990
Like why are they watching it?

00:09:14.990 --> 00:09:19.620
It’s the weirdest thing when you go into
a play or into a theater, people are watching

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:26.660
people on an imaginary stage, we’re going
to watch people act out life.

00:09:26.660 --> 00:09:29.040
It doesn’t make any…it’s like bizarre!

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:34.090
But the reason why people are doing it is
because it gives them meaning for their struggles.

00:09:34.090 --> 00:09:36.010
They go “I’ve done that.

00:09:36.010 --> 00:09:37.510
That’s me.

00:09:37.510 --> 00:09:41.720
I’ve fought Cancer.

00:09:41.720 --> 00:09:42.720
Something happened to me.

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:44.210
I won the State Championship.

00:09:44.210 --> 00:09:47.160
I want to be able to win the State Championship.

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:48.920
I want to be able to come from nowhere.

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:50.230
I want to believe.

00:09:50.230 --> 00:09:51.920
I want to fall in love.

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:55.220
I want to survive the loss of a loved one.

00:09:55.220 --> 00:09:58.190
This can happen, I saw a story.

00:09:58.190 --> 00:10:03.290
I mean this is why, it gives us context so
we’re not walking around going “Why am

00:10:03.290 --> 00:10:04.590
I doing all of this?”

00:10:04.590 --> 00:10:08.840
You see the movie and you’re like “Other
people have been dumped!

00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:10.600
Other people have been fired!

00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:14.290
Other people have been discriminated against,
there has been injustice.

00:10:14.290 --> 00:10:16.970
I’ve suffered injustice, so have they.

00:10:16.970 --> 00:10:18.240
I can live.

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:19.740
I’m going to fight.

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:21.350
That’s why people go to movies.

00:10:21.350 --> 00:10:23.200
That’s the only thing anybody cares about.

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:29.480
They don’t care that the point-of-no-return
happens on page 63 or some crap.

00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:31.900
You’re writing from that place.

00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:33.580
That’s no punk rock!

00:10:33.580 --> 00:10:35.000
That’s just stories.

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:36.410
That’s storytelling.

00:10:36.410 --> 00:10:43.880
That’s conservative, liberal, it’s not
supposed to be political but I’m just saying

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:44.880
that’s anybody.

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:47.160
That’s any ethos, that is the ethos.

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:54.210
So if you’re looking to get in the business,
the young lady in the front row, the person

00:10:54.210 --> 00:11:01.700
who gives the apple to the teacher, this is
the business of emotion.

00:11:01.700 --> 00:11:10.350
It’s the business of human identification
with authentic stories and that’s heavy

00:11:10.350 --> 00:11:11.350
lifting.

00:11:11.350 --> 00:11:15.630
That requires sharing of your life experience,
it’s demanding.

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:21.250
It’s just as much as a surgeon, it’s emotionally
demanding to be a nurse.

00:11:21.250 --> 00:11:25.420
It’s emotionally demanding to be a trash
man because you’re looking at people’s

00:11:25.420 --> 00:11:26.420
trash and you’re like “Oh my gosh!

00:11:26.420 --> 00:11:27.910
They threw out this photo album?”

00:11:27.910 --> 00:11:31.490
It’s like everybody has got to deal with
something.

00:11:31.490 --> 00:11:36.380
This is the thing, this is that business.

00:11:36.380 --> 00:11:45.320
So you can’t get around that and when people
try to get around that then the great actor

00:11:45.320 --> 00:11:46.430
becomes the good actor.

00:11:46.430 --> 00:11:49.490
And then the great screenplay becomes an okay
movie.

00:11:49.490 --> 00:11:55.430
But when you go there and you sell the whole
thing and you don’t shy away, then you’re

00:11:55.430 --> 00:12:02.510
Kirosawa, then you’re Eugene O’Neill,
then you’re doing 12 YEARS OF SLAVE, then

00:12:02.510 --> 00:12:07.110
you’re fearless, then you’re telling this,
you’re doing this thing that people go “Ahh!

00:12:07.110 --> 00:12:08.110
That’s awesome.”

00:12:08.110 --> 00:12:14.300
BLACK KLANSMANN, like an amazing, honest movie
about the truth.

00:12:14.300 --> 00:12:23.510
It’s a great movie and it’s about the
truth and it’s emotional, it’s disturbing.

00:12:23.510 --> 00:12:27.910
But that’s Shakespeare, that’s never changed.

00:12:27.910 --> 00:12:35.370
That’s not new, that’s not avant-garde
and audiences are still responding to it.

00:12:35.370 --> 00:12:39.600
Because we’re human beings and we still
respond to a good story.

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:47.250
Mark: *that usually ends up being 12-15 pages
as the outline in my experience with my producers.

00:12:47.250 --> 00:12:54.940
And that outline is then rewritten sometimes
two or three times, I get notes on the outline

00:12:54.940 --> 00:12:56.530
before I’m allowed to go to the script.

00:12:56.530 --> 00:13:02.780
So the script is the final…once we okay
the outline, then go to pages.

00:13:02.780 --> 00:13:07.340
And that’s not to say they don’t change
their mind once the script is finished because

00:13:07.340 --> 00:13:09.790
that’s the crazy part…

00:13:09.790 --> 00:13:12.370
“Yeah but you agreed to it in the outline?”

00:13:12.370 --> 00:13:17.030
But we built the house but now we don’t
kind of like the way it looks.

00:13:17.030 --> 00:13:18.820
You know we thought we did.

00:13:18.820 --> 00:13:20.290
So that happens, too.

00:13:20.290 --> 00:13:28.080
To be open and prepared for rewrites at all
stages it does allow you to write a faster

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:30.790
first draft is to have a solid outline.

00:13:30.790 --> 00:13:35.040
Because for me I have to see the film in my
head before I can write it.

00:13:35.040 --> 00:13:39.530
And I know that I have a lot of problems ahead
of me if I can’t see it.

00:13:39.530 --> 00:13:43.970
I don’t lock in, like I’ve almost seen
the movie.

00:13:43.970 --> 00:13:45.750
Like when you watch a movie, you remember
it.

00:13:45.750 --> 00:13:47.540
I have to see it that way.

00:13:47.540 --> 00:13:51.150
And I know things are working.

00:13:51.150 --> 00:13:54.400
And you know the outline is good for living
with your characters.

00:13:54.400 --> 00:13:56.210
You get to live with them.

00:13:56.210 --> 00:14:01.680
You get to see how the movie is working or
doesn’t work before you sit down and write

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:06.300
that screenplay, which is building the house
(which is almost at the pre-blueprint) in

00:14:06.300 --> 00:14:07.750
my opinion, that’s been my experience.

00:14:07.750 --> 00:14:12.220
But I know writers who just want to write
down a couple of lines and wing it.

00:14:12.220 --> 00:14:19.900
There are so many things that can go wrong
and why not turn in the most amazing first

00:14:19.900 --> 00:14:20.900
draft you can?

00:14:20.900 --> 00:14:23.040
I don’t see the problem with that.

00:14:23.040 --> 00:14:27.110
And like I say, you have to when you’re
doing assignment work.

00:14:27.110 --> 00:14:28.850
You want fewer drafts.

00:14:28.850 --> 00:14:31.850
You don’t want eight drafts because nothing
is working.

00:14:31.850 --> 00:14:34.070
You want to turn that in where they go “Wow!”

00:14:34.070 --> 00:14:36.390
One of my assignments I did two drafts and
two polishes.

00:14:36.390 --> 00:14:37.390
We’re done!

00:14:37.390 --> 00:14:38.520
That’s what they like.

00:14:38.520 --> 00:14:41.520
They don’t 5 [drafts] “You’re not getting
it…”

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:46.470
That’s where you get fired and they hire
someone else who can facilitate the notes

00:14:46.470 --> 00:14:49.590
and get it moving.

00:14:49.590 --> 00:14:51.540
It’s creative but it’s also a business
at the time, unfortunately.

